    {'para': '**137.** Neuter adjectives (but not substantives) in ⟨u⟩ may take the forms proper to the masculine in the dat., abl.-gen., loc. sing., and gen.-loc. dual.'},
    {'para': '**138. Changes of final ⟨n⟩.** Before initial ⟨j⟩ and ⟨ś⟩, ⟨n⟩ becomes ⟨ñ⟩; thus, ⟨tān janān⟩ becomes ⟨tāñ janān⟩; ⟨tān śatrūn⟩ = ⟨tāñ śatrūn⟩. In last case, however, ⟨ch⟩ is almost always substituted for the initial ⟨ś⟩; thus, ⟨tāñ chatrūn⟩.'},
    {'para': '**139.** Final ⟨n⟩, before an initial ⟨l⟩, is assimilated and becomes nasalized ⟨l⟩, which is written ल्ँ [⟨ṃl⟩], or (what is the same thing) anusvāra ⟨ṃ⟩; thus ⟨tān lokān⟩ becomes ⟨tāṃl lokān⟩ or ⟨tāṃ lokān⟩.'},
    {'para': '**140.** Before the surd palatal, lingual, and dental mutes there is inserted after final ⟨n⟩ a sibilant of each of those classes respectively, before which ⟨n⟩ becomes *anusvāra*; thus for ⟨tān ca⟩ we find ⟨tāṃś ca⟩; for ⟨tān tathā⟩, ⟨tāṃs tathā⟩.∗'},
